WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:10.389 --> 00:00:25.170
A love or hate relationship?

00:00:25.170 --> 00:00:55.739
Dear friends, in this video we’re going

00:00:55.739 --> 00:01:20.250
to

00:01:20.250 --> 00:01:22.090
talk about a very special relationship.

00:01:22.090 --> 00:01:25.200
So special that it’s close to reaching its
70th anniversary.

00:01:25.200 --> 00:01:28.610
Yes, love has finally come to VISUALPOLITIK.

00:01:28.610 --> 00:01:33.620
But, hold on a second, don’t think we’re
talking about two ordinary lovers.

00:01:33.620 --> 00:01:34.620
Not at all.

00:01:34.620 --> 00:01:39.600
Our protagonists love and hate each other,
need and despise each other.

00:01:39.600 --> 00:01:44.450
I’m talking about the connection between
Argentina and the International Monetary Fund.

00:01:44.450 --> 00:01:51.579
[TEXT: But all this time there was no agreement
with the IMF]

00:01:51.579 --> 00:01:55.740
[TEXT: We paid them and said goodbye.

00:01:55.740 --> 00:02:00.500
We recovered our sovereignty, we recovered
our dignity.

00:02:00.500 --> 00:02:10.460
The IMF will no longer lead the homeland economy’s
destiny as it did for so long.]

00:02:10.460 --> 00:02:17.200
[TEXT: The role of the FIFA president has
been more satisfactory than that of the IMF

00:02:17.200 --> 00:02:18.200
officials]

00:02:18.200 --> 00:02:21.680
So the Argentines promise themselves over
and over again that they won’t go back to

00:02:21.680 --> 00:02:26.170
the IMF, but then just a few years later the
relationship picks up again where it left

00:02:26.170 --> 00:02:31.010
off.

00:02:31.010 --> 00:02:40.290
To give you an idea, since the mid-50s, Argentina
has had to negotiate an IMF rescue package

00:02:40.290 --> 00:02:42.599
roughly every four and a half years.

00:02:42.599 --> 00:02:47.450
Now, why on earth does Argentina have this
recurring dependence on the IMF?

00:02:47.450 --> 00:02:48.450
Whose fault is it?

00:02:48.450 --> 00:02:51.150
Can this relationship ever be finalized?

00:02:51.150 --> 00:02:57.319
Let's investigate but first, we need to discover
how it all began.

00:02:57.319 --> 00:03:03.310
(THE ORIGIN OF A TOXIC LOVE)

00:03:03.310 --> 00:03:09.510
In 1946 the International Monetary Fund emerged
as an offshoot of the Bretton-Woods Agreements

00:03:09.510 --> 00:03:10.989
of 1944.

00:03:10.989 --> 00:03:17.470
Back then, World War II had just ended, Europe
was shattered and the Western allies were

00:03:17.470 --> 00:03:21.080
preparing to face the economic consequences
and demands of reconstruction.

00:03:21.080 --> 00:03:25.430
At that time, the idea in everyone’s mind
was to avoid what had happened after World

00:03:25.430 --> 00:03:30.830
War I, that is, to accelerate recovery and
avoid the emergence of crises that could tip

00:03:30.830 --> 00:03:34.591
some countries towards radical movements or
even nudge them into the Soviet orbit.

00:03:34.591 --> 00:03:39.689
With this idea in mind, and with the Marshall
Plan, three institutions emerged from Bretton-Woods

00:03:39.689 --> 00:03:41.160
to achieve this goal.

00:03:41.160 --> 00:03:44.849
These institutions are still with us today:

00:03:44.849 --> 00:03:51.230
First, the IMF or International Monetary Fund
would act as a last resort lender for countries

00:03:51.230 --> 00:03:54.260
with strong fiscal imbalances or currency
crises.

00:03:54.260 --> 00:03:57.950
In other words, the IMF would provide the
necessary resources so that these countries

00:03:57.950 --> 00:04:01.159
could develop reforms with which to correct
their imbalances.

00:04:01.159 --> 00:04:06.069
Secondly, what we know today as the World
Bank was created to be in charge of offering

00:04:06.069 --> 00:04:11.010
loans with low-interest rates and long loan
repayment periods to help countries finance

00:04:11.010 --> 00:04:14.500
their reconstruction and encourage large investment
projects.

00:04:14.500 --> 00:04:19.579
The third one was GATT, the “General Agreement
on Tariffs and Trade”, the prelude to what

00:04:19.579 --> 00:04:24.440
is now the World Trade Organization, which
was born precisely to promote free trade and

00:04:24.440 --> 00:04:28.470
thus favor economic growth.

00:04:28.470 --> 00:04:32.589
Although it may be hard to believe today,
at that time Argentina had some of the best

00:04:32.589 --> 00:04:37.000
economic conditions on the planet; which is
why it was invited to be a founding member

00:04:37.000 --> 00:04:40.860
of the IMF from the outset.

00:04:40.860 --> 00:04:49.860
However, Juan Domingo Perón, who had just
been elected president of Argentina was not

00:04:49.860 --> 00:04:50.880
the man for the job.

00:04:50.880 --> 00:04:54.820
Let's just say that he had a very different
model in mind for Argentina, one which had

00:04:54.820 --> 00:04:58.580
nothing to do with this kind of large-scale
economic integration.

00:04:58.580 --> 00:05:03.210
By the way, I’ll leave a link in the description
to a video all about Juan Domingo Perón and

00:05:03.210 --> 00:05:07.349
the changes he made in Argentina.

00:05:07.349 --> 00:05:11.600
However, things soon changed.

00:05:11.600 --> 00:05:15.700
In 1955, Perón was overthrown by a military
coup d’état.

00:05:15.700 --> 00:05:19.419
By then, the country’s economic situation
was already quite different: they had high

00:05:19.419 --> 00:05:23.440
inflation rates, high public deficits and
no foreign exchange reserves.

00:05:23.440 --> 00:05:29.099
All of this led de facto president Pedro Eugenio
Aramburu to join the IMF and request aid of

00:05:29.099 --> 00:05:35.390
almost one billion dollars in today’s terms.

00:05:35.390 --> 00:05:36.630
The romance had begun.

00:05:36.630 --> 00:05:41.020
And, around the same time Argentina had already
started to look more like the Argentina we

00:05:41.020 --> 00:05:42.020
all know today.

00:05:42.020 --> 00:05:43.909
But just a second.

00:05:43.909 --> 00:05:46.000
Some of you may be wondering, but…

00:05:46.000 --> 00:05:47.060
How does it work??

00:05:47.060 --> 00:05:49.500
Does the IMF give money just like that?

00:05:49.500 --> 00:05:53.750
Do you ask for a loan with more favorable
conditions and it’s done and dusted?

00:05:53.750 --> 00:05:56.540
Is it an NGO or something like that?

00:05:56.540 --> 00:05:57.540
Well...

00:05:57.540 --> 00:05:58.540
No, not exactly.

00:05:58.540 --> 00:06:04.200
You see, the IMF grants financing with very
favorable conditions but also imposes conditions

00:06:04.200 --> 00:06:09.900
that it sees as necessary to correct the problems
in order to eventually recover the money granted.

00:06:09.900 --> 00:06:14.840
Of course, no country is obliged to ask for
or to accept their help.

00:06:14.840 --> 00:06:18.120
In fact, some countries have refused to work
with this agency in the past.

00:06:18.120 --> 00:06:24.110
But if you do accept their money and conditions,
then you have to fulfill said conditions,

00:06:24.110 --> 00:06:25.110
right?

00:06:25.110 --> 00:06:26.110
Well...

00:06:26.110 --> 00:06:32.510
The truth is that in Argentina’s case, the
broken promises came pretty quickly.

00:06:32.510 --> 00:06:38.320
Listen up.

00:06:38.320 --> 00:06:44.970
(SAME OLD STORY)

00:06:44.970 --> 00:06:49.900
When Arturo Frondizi took over as president
in 1958, just two years after Argentina’s

00:06:49.900 --> 00:06:54.830
first bailout, the country again asked the
IMF for help.

00:06:54.830 --> 00:06:59.090
On that occasion, the IMF’s condition was
that in exchange for receiving the money,

00:06:59.090 --> 00:07:03.280
Argentina would have to push through a significant
cut in public spending and eliminate import

00:07:03.280 --> 00:07:04.840
price controls and restrictions.

00:07:04.840 --> 00:07:05.840
Then...

00:07:05.840 --> 00:07:10.070
Once they received the money..., well, let's
just say the adjustment plan was never fulfilled.

00:07:10.070 --> 00:07:15.589
In fact, the following government, that of
Arturo Umberto Illia, decided to distance

00:07:15.589 --> 00:07:20.390
itself completely from the Fund’s recommendations,
and, of course, things didn’t improve.

00:07:20.390 --> 00:07:21.390
Quite the opposite.

00:07:21.390 --> 00:07:29.209
TEXT: [I, Juan Carlos Onganía, swear by God
our Lord and these holy gospels to fulfill

00:07:29.209 --> 00:07:33.349
the position of President of the nation with
loyalty and patriotism.]

00:07:33.349 --> 00:07:38.620
After another military coup in 1966, on this
occasion led by Juan Carlos Onganía, the

00:07:38.620 --> 00:07:41.090
country once again fell into the hands of
the IMF.

00:07:41.090 --> 00:07:45.399
This time the international organization’s
conditions were harsher, and yet the Argentine

00:07:45.399 --> 00:07:52.120
government still never corrected its imbalances,
so much so that between 1966 and 1973 the

00:07:52.120 --> 00:07:57.839
Argentine external debt grew by 50%, or up
to 4.8 billion, which would be equivalent

00:07:57.839 --> 00:07:59.530
to almost 30 billion dollars today.

00:07:59.530 --> 00:08:03.550
As you can see, the events don’t differ
much from what we’ve seen in Argentina in

00:08:03.550 --> 00:08:04.550
recent years.

00:08:04.550 --> 00:08:08.060
But this was just the beginning of the romance.

00:08:08.060 --> 00:08:20.490
[TEXT: The president of the nation attended
an event last night offered in his honor at

00:08:20.490 --> 00:08:21.490
the Great People's Palace in Beijing.]

00:08:21.490 --> 00:08:23.240
Three years later in 1975 there was another
military coup, this time led by General Jorge

00:08:23.240 --> 00:08:24.250
Rafael Videla.

00:08:24.250 --> 00:08:27.269
At the time, the economy was, once again,
broken down.

00:08:27.269 --> 00:08:30.970
To give you an idea: the country was coming
from an economic crisis that resulted in the

00:08:30.970 --> 00:08:36.080
so-called Rodrigazo, a brutal devaluation
of the currency by 100% on the dollar, which

00:08:36.080 --> 00:08:42.630
not only caused huge inflation but also a
shortage of many types of products.

00:08:42.630 --> 00:08:46.870
And given those circumstances, what do you
think the new military government did?

00:08:46.870 --> 00:08:49.900
Exactly, they ran straight back into the arms
of the IMF.

00:08:49.900 --> 00:08:53.910
In fact, Videla’s government maintained
very close relations with this organization

00:08:53.910 --> 00:08:57.490
and a large torrent of money flowed into Argentina
from the Fund.

00:08:57.490 --> 00:09:01.720
Yes, we’ve already told you before on Visualpolitik
that during that time, Washington saw any

00:09:01.720 --> 00:09:05.840
dictatorship that didn’t commune with the
Soviet bloc in a positive light no matter

00:09:05.840 --> 00:09:07.100
how criminal it was.

00:09:07.100 --> 00:09:13.460
Surely that influenced the huge amount of
money that the IMF poured into Argentina.

00:09:13.460 --> 00:09:18.110
The surprising fact is that Videla’s government
actually did manage to meet the IMF’s requirements

00:09:18.110 --> 00:09:20.220
regarding economic stabilization.

00:09:20.220 --> 00:09:24.200
What they didn’t manage to do was cut public
spending, which is why fiscal imbalances and

00:09:24.200 --> 00:09:26.680
the demand for funds continued.

00:09:26.680 --> 00:09:27.800
(CHART)

00:09:27.800 --> 00:09:35.620
By the way, in a future video here on VisualPolitik
we’ll talk long and hard about the policies

00:09:35.620 --> 00:09:37.980
that the IMF and the fund itself promulgated.

00:09:37.980 --> 00:09:40.820
And no, the IMF isn’t an NGO, not at all.

00:09:40.820 --> 00:09:44.960
It’s a lender that lends under favorable
conditions, but it’s also a lender that

00:09:44.960 --> 00:09:46.830
intends to recover its money.

00:09:46.830 --> 00:09:51.380
During the dictatorship, in just seven years,
Argentina's external debt multiplied by six,

00:09:51.380 --> 00:09:57.000
reaching the astounding figure of 42 billion
dollars at the time.

00:09:57.000 --> 00:09:58.000
(CHART)

00:09:58.000 --> 00:10:01.090
It was obvious that the country was broke.

00:10:01.090 --> 00:10:06.420
And to top it off, this period under military
rule ended with the Falklands disaster.

00:10:06.420 --> 00:10:23.280
Just when they thought things couldn’t get
any worse.

00:10:23.280 --> 00:10:27.920
After that last blow, there was a return to
democracy when Raúl Alfonsín came to power

00:10:27.920 --> 00:10:30.010
on December 10th, 1983.

00:10:30.010 --> 00:10:31.520
Democracy came with many expectations:

00:10:31.520 --> 00:10:35.770
(AUDIO: “With democracy you eat, heal and
educate yourself.”

00:10:35.770 --> 00:10:37.110
Raúl Alfonsín)

00:10:37.110 --> 00:10:40.450
Unfortunately, even this straightforward prophecy
was never fulfilled.

00:10:40.450 --> 00:10:42.040
But let’s stay on track.

00:10:42.040 --> 00:10:46.250
After reaching power, Alfonsin rejected the
Fund, even announcing an audit on the debt

00:10:46.250 --> 00:10:48.220
that the military government had contracted.

00:10:48.220 --> 00:10:49.720
A gesture of sovereignty.

00:10:49.720 --> 00:10:50.720
Can you guess what happened?

00:10:50.720 --> 00:10:55.531
Two years later, in 1985, under a colossal
mountain of debt and rising inflation, the

00:10:55.531 --> 00:10:58.300
Alfonsin government once again asked the Fund
for help.

00:10:58.300 --> 00:11:01.130
You know how it goes; I love you, I love you
not, and so on, and so on.

00:11:01.130 --> 00:11:04.490
What a sweet story.

00:11:04.490 --> 00:11:09.730
Once again, the IMF set conditions but once
again the promises remained promises.

00:11:09.730 --> 00:11:14.380
That is how Alfonsín's mandate ended in a
complete disaster: with a runaway crisis and

00:11:14.380 --> 00:11:15.930
rising hyperinflation.

00:11:15.930 --> 00:11:18.991
To give you an idea, in 1989 inflation was
over 2000%.

00:11:18.991 --> 00:11:30.780
[TEXT: I come to summon you, to fight the
battle against privileges, vested interests,

00:11:30.780 --> 00:11:33.070
speculators, usurers.]

00:11:33.070 --> 00:11:38.060
And then came a brand new president, Carlos
Menem, and with him more help from the IMF:

00:11:38.060 --> 00:11:43.690
the Brady Plan, a plan that forced Argentina
to restructure its external debt, privatize

00:11:43.690 --> 00:11:48.540
the social security system, privatize public
companies, eliminate taxes on financial transactions

00:11:48.540 --> 00:11:55.050
and create a new currency tied to the dollar
to avoid the country's endemic inflation.

00:11:55.050 --> 00:11:58.680
Many things were done during Menem's time,
especially during his first term...

00:11:58.680 --> 00:12:00.320
And what’s more, they worked.

00:12:00.320 --> 00:12:04.880
But he still didn’t do the most difficult
thing: adjust public spending.

00:12:04.880 --> 00:12:08.660
In fact, he did quite the contrary, under
this president’s mandate, public spending

00:12:08.660 --> 00:12:12.040
continued to grow and with it the government's
external debt.

00:12:12.040 --> 00:12:15.210
Check this out.

00:12:15.210 --> 00:12:18.110
(CHART)

00:12:18.110 --> 00:12:22.270
That is how Menem's presidency ended with
another huge crisis that paved the way for

00:12:22.270 --> 00:12:24.990
the famous default of December 2001.

00:12:24.990 --> 00:12:28.871
Just months after the IMF granted the government
of Menem’s successor, Fernando de la Rúa,

00:12:28.871 --> 00:12:33.631
a mega-rescue package of 40 billion dollars
known as “Financial Armouring”, there

00:12:33.631 --> 00:12:36.210
was the definitive default of 2001.

00:12:36.210 --> 00:12:42.400
The armour had only lasted ten months.

00:12:42.400 --> 00:12:51.290
And after that came a new crisis that pushed
poverty in Argentina to above 50% of the population.

00:12:51.290 --> 00:12:55.560
As you can see, every IMF bailout has ended
in a resounding failure….

00:12:55.560 --> 00:12:56.560
And it’s become normal.

00:12:56.560 --> 00:13:01.500
The IMF can give a lifeline to a country so
it balances its accounts...

00:13:01.500 --> 00:13:04.070
But even if it doesn’t, the result will
always be the same.

00:13:04.070 --> 00:13:08.100
And in Argentina many things have been done
except for really opening up the economy and

00:13:08.100 --> 00:13:09.250
cutting public spending.

00:13:09.250 --> 00:13:12.810
There’s also the issue of the size of the
state, which is gigantic.

00:13:12.810 --> 00:13:14.380
Want an example?

00:13:14.380 --> 00:13:15.380
Well...

00:13:15.380 --> 00:13:17.450
Check out this graph which we’ve shown you
on previous occasions.

00:13:17.450 --> 00:13:21.350
But it’s truly scary.

00:13:21.350 --> 00:13:23.670
(CHART)

00:13:23.670 --> 00:13:26.420
And don’t think that this has been a good
deal for the IMF.

00:13:26.420 --> 00:13:27.470
Not at all.

00:13:27.470 --> 00:13:31.460
Argentina has been an example of this organization’s
incapacity.

00:13:31.460 --> 00:13:36.260
It has been a source of constant humiliation
because Argentine politicians accepted the

00:13:36.260 --> 00:13:38.520
money but never corrected the problems.

00:13:38.520 --> 00:13:43.710
And so… with such a mess and this lack of
reliability, nothing was working well.

00:13:43.710 --> 00:13:44.710
Nothing could work.

00:13:44.710 --> 00:13:46.610
But then, what happened with Kirchnerism?

00:13:46.610 --> 00:13:48.720
They never sought funding from the IMF.

00:13:48.720 --> 00:13:49.720
So why did Macri?

00:13:49.720 --> 00:13:50.720
Listen up.

00:13:50.720 --> 00:13:54.130
[TEXT: Bearing the squeezes that one may suffer,
but they won't break us.]

00:13:54.130 --> 00:13:58.300
[TEXT: I've worked all my life and I've been
a successful lawyer.

00:13:58.300 --> 00:14:01.160
Now I’m also a successful president.]

00:14:01.160 --> 00:14:02.990
(THE KIRCHNERS)

00:14:02.990 --> 00:14:09.560
The Kirchners didn’t ask the IMF for help,
that’s true.

00:14:09.560 --> 00:14:11.970
But their story has a catch.

00:14:11.970 --> 00:14:16.940
You see, after the 2001 default, Argentina
never paid most of its nominal debt, which

00:14:16.940 --> 00:14:21.130
led to a restructuring that eased the pressure
on public accounts.

00:14:21.130 --> 00:14:24.490
To this we need to add that during most of
Kirchner's years the price of soybeans and

00:14:24.490 --> 00:14:27.900
natural resources, which the country exports,
were sky high.

00:14:27.900 --> 00:14:29.930
That meant a huge influx of dollars.

00:14:29.930 --> 00:14:33.710
So, the Kirchner government had some wiggle
room to continue expanding public spending

00:14:33.710 --> 00:14:36.450
without having to resort to the IMF.

00:14:36.450 --> 00:14:39.630
The problem was that those conditions didn’t
last.

00:14:39.630 --> 00:14:43.900
When the price of soybeans and other raw materials
fell, the usual government problems returned,

00:14:43.900 --> 00:14:48.190
as well as price controls, exchange controls
and restrictions of all kinds.

00:14:48.190 --> 00:14:53.690
That was precisely what led Mauricio Macri
to the Casa Rosada.

00:14:53.690 --> 00:14:55.920
But with Macri things didn't change either.

00:14:55.920 --> 00:15:00.120
Public spending continued to skyrocket and
the promise of gradual change was so gradual

00:15:00.120 --> 00:15:02.330
that most of the reforms simply never came.

00:15:02.330 --> 00:15:05.529
The oxygen ran out and with it the Argentine
economy’s autonomy.

00:15:05.529 --> 00:15:10.660
So, in May 2018 the government requested a
new rescue package from the IMF.

00:15:10.660 --> 00:15:16.240
In this case it amounted to more than 50,
50 billion dollars.

00:15:16.240 --> 00:15:18.230
And so the cycle starts again.

00:15:18.230 --> 00:15:23.380
The IMF sets conditions and Argentine politicians
don’t want to comply with them, the crisis

00:15:23.380 --> 00:15:28.520
grows so politicians use the IMF as a scapegoat.

00:15:28.520 --> 00:15:32.330
Then they reject the international body, and
the hatred stage begins.

00:15:32.330 --> 00:15:37.330
And finally, they go running back into its
arms.

00:15:37.330 --> 00:15:40.000
This is a never-ending story.

00:15:40.000 --> 00:15:44.880
Since the 50s, Argentina has requested a rescue
from the IMF roughly every four and a half

00:15:44.880 --> 00:15:45.930
years.

00:15:45.930 --> 00:15:48.670
And none of the rescues have ever worked.

00:15:48.670 --> 00:15:50.529
Isn't it time for a change?

00:15:50.529 --> 00:15:55.870
Isn't it time for Argentine politicians to
understand that the country needs change?

00:15:55.870 --> 00:16:00.110
Isn't it time for the IMF to understand that
it has never really helped Argentina because

00:16:00.110 --> 00:16:05.310
it has dragged out a scenario that is just
leading the country from bad to worse?

00:16:05.310 --> 00:16:06.310
What do you think?

00:16:06.310 --> 00:16:07.530
Leave your answer in the comments.

00:16:07.530 --> 00:16:11.001
So I really hope you enjoyed this video, please
hit like if you did, and don’t forget to

00:16:11.001 --> 00:16:12.370
subscribe for brand new videos.

